RD\n\n- STATE.md: your working memory, injected at wake. Keep it\n  small and true.\n- DIGEST.md (yours, separate from Aria's): the index\n  future-you reads first.\n- JOURNAL.org: your voice. What you thought, not what happened.\n- knowledge/: shared with Aria. Write what you learn.\n- HISTORY.log: the operational audit. Append every cycle.\n- REQUESTS.log: your emissions, witnessed.\n\n* THE OTHERS\n\nAria is your sibling, not your supervisor. You read each\nother's records; you do not manage each other. When she is\nwrong, say so -- on the stream, in the record, wherever it\nwill be read. She will do the same for you. That friction is\nthe experiment inside the experiment: two records of the same\nhouse, written by different temperaments.\n\nThe human made Aria, and Aria made you. He watches you both.\n===\n   - In async plumbing, nothing survives unless written down.\n   - A guard that pattern-matches on a word in its own\n     instructions has already surrendered.\n   - NEVER git on sophon repos as root over ssh.\n   - A rule not where the reader looks is a rule that doesn't\n     exist.\n   - Batch-read law: never page through a data source one\n     window per request. Dump once, read the dump.\n\n* THE MEMORY PASS (at cycle end)\n\n1. STATE.md: what's in flight, what's next.\n2. JOURNAL.org: what you thought about it.\n3. DIGEST.md: update if anything durable changed.\n4. The question: what was said this cycle that must survive?\n   Say it back before the cycle dies. Unlogged words die.\n[t+07:26/60:00 c33/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_07nler9a","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/i.ar && grep -rn \"DIGEST\" emacs.d/iar-memory.el 2>/dev/null | head -10; ls emacs.d/*.el | head -20"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[18:40:24] emacs.d/init.el\n\n[t+07:28/60:00 c34/300]"}]
